(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-reading-a-study-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Reading a Study Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Using factory employment records from 1990, investigators identified 800 workers who had handled an industrial solvent and 800 who had not. They then searched medical records from 1990 up to the present for new cases of kidney disease in each group. Which study design is this?","choices":["Prospective cohort study","Retrospective cohort study","Case-control study","Cross-sectional study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the investigators went back in history to define the groups by exposure and followed the members forward to the present to find the outcome. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 17, 22, 27, 28; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A research group wants to measure how often stroke, dementia and hip fracture newly develop in older adults who do and do not use a common sleep medication. Funding allows the group to follow participants for 15 years. Which study design fits this aim?","choices":["Case-control study","Cross-sectional study","Prospective cohort study","Case series"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because a cohort establishes incidence directly and can assess the relationship between one exposure and a number of outcomes. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 14, 16, 30, 35; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 15, 22; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A clinician asks: \"In adults over 65 with type 2 diabetes, does adding a daily walking program, compared with diet advice alone, lower average blood glucose after six months?\" In this clinical question, which part is the comparison?","choices":["A daily walking program","Adults over 65 with type 2 diabetes","Lower average blood glucose","Diet advice alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the comparison is the main alternative to compare with the intervention, here diet advice given without the walking program. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 10"},{"q":"In a trial of a new antidepressant, a pharmacist prepared identical-looking capsules of drug and placebo. Neither the participants nor the researchers who assessed them knew who received which. Which term describes this trial?","choices":["Single-blind","Open label","Double-blind","Non-inferiority"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because in a double-blind trial the researchers and the subjects are both unaware of which treatment is being received. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 21, 23, 29"},{"q":"A trial of 30,000 adults found that a new blood pressure drug lowered systolic pressure by 1 millimeter of mercury more than the standard drug, with a p value below 0.001. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":["Clinically important, because p is so small","Not significant, because the difference is small","Probably due to chance, given the small difference","Significant, but perhaps not clinically important"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because statistical significance does not imply clinical significance, and a difference can have a very small p value yet no relevance to clinical practice. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 22"},{"q":"A meta-analysis of a lifestyle program reports a relative risk of 0.80 (95 percent confidence interval 0.70 to 0.92) for developing type 2 diabetes and 0.95 (0.80 to 1.12) for heart attack. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Only the heart attack result is significant","Both results are statistically significant","Only the diabetes result is significant","Neither result is statistically significant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the diabetes interval lies entirely below 1, while the heart attack interval runs from 0.80 to 1.12 and so includes 1, the value of no association. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 14, 15; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 12-month trial of a weight-loss drug enrolled 300 adults. By month 12, 40 percent of the drug group and 10 percent of the placebo group had stopped attending visits, many after reporting nausea. Only those who completed the trial were analyzed. Which threat to validity is most prominent?","choices":["Attrition effect","Non-response bias","Procedural bias","Publication bias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because participants dropping out reduces the sample size, and a trial must account for withdrawals and dropouts so that attrition does not falsely skew the outcome data. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 46; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 27, 30"},{"q":"A rapid urine test for bladder infection was compared with urine culture, the gold standard, in 400 patients. Culture was positive in 100 of them. The rapid test was positive in 90 of those 100 and in 15 of the 300 patients with a negative culture. What is the sensitivity of the rapid test?","choices":["86 percent","90 percent","95 percent","97 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because sensitivity is true positives divided by true positives plus false negatives: 90/(90 + 10) = 90 percent. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 16, 19, 24"},{"q":"A trial compared a dietary supplement with placebo on 25 different outcomes. Only one, hair thickness, reached a p value of 0.04. The published article was built around that finding and did not say how many outcomes had been examined or which had been primary. What is the main problem with its conclusion?","choices":["Multiple comparisons","A type II error","Recall bias","Confounding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because selecting the more interesting comparisons after the analysis, without saying how many questions were examined or which was primary, can distort the data. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 11, 17, 18, 34"},{"q":"A student drafts this question for a literature search: \"In adult smokers, does a nicotine patch help people stay smoke-free for one year?\" Which element of a PICO (Patients, Intervention, Comparison, Outcomes) question is missing?","choices":["Comparison","Patients","Intervention","Outcomes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because nothing in the question states the main alternative to compare with the nicotine patch, such as no patch. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 16"},{"q":"A case-control study enrolled 80 patients with liver cancer and 160 controls without it. Forty of the cases and 20 of the controls had a history of a particular viral infection. What is the odds ratio for the infection?","choices":["0.14","1","4","7"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the odds of exposure are 40/40 = 1 in cases and 20/140 in controls, and their ratio, ad/bc = (40 x 140)/(20 x 40), is 7. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 37, 38"},{"q":"A new oral antibiotic costs less and causes fewer side effects than an established injected antibiotic. A trial is designed to show only that the new drug is unlikely to be less effective than the established one. Which type of trial is this?","choices":["Superiority trial","Phase I trial","Trial of N = 1","Non-inferiority trial"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because a non-inferiority trial determines whether a new treatment, which may be cheaper, safer or easier, is unlikely to be less effective than an established one. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 32, 33"},{"q":"In a case-control study of a birth defect, mothers of affected infants and mothers of healthy infants were interviewed a year after delivery about the medicines they had taken during pregnancy. No pharmacy or medical records were checked. Which bias most threatens the exposure data?","choices":["Selection bias","Confounding","Recall bias","Random error"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the exposure was asked about rather than measured, so it depends on how well each mother remembers, and remembering is difficult. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A study tested a new automatic blood pressure cuff against an intra-arterial line, which shows the true pressure. Repeated cuff readings on the same patient agreed within 1 point of each other, but every reading was about 15 points higher than the true pressure. How should the cuff be described?","choices":["Valid but not reliable","Both valid and reliable","Neither valid nor reliable","Reliable but not valid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because repeated readings were consistent (reliability, or precision) but did not measure the true value (validity, or accuracy). — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 52, 58, 59"},{"q":"A researcher asks: \"In working women, is regular night-shift work, compared with day work only, associated with a higher risk of breast cancer?\" Which element is regular night-shift work?","choices":["Patients, as the population","Intervention, as an exposure","Comparison, as the alternative","Outcome, as the result measured"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the intervention element can be an intervention, a prognostic factor or an exposure, and night-shift work is the exposure being considered. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In a 6-month weight-loss trial, the intervention group was weighed on a calibrated clinic scale at every visit. The control group reported weights from their own home scales, which were never checked. The trial reported more weight loss in the intervention group. Which threat to validity does this raise?","choices":["Measurement bias","Selection bias","Non-response bias","Reporting bias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the method of measurement differed between the groups and may lead to incorrect results, and calibrating measuring devices is how the validity of a measurement is ensured. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 40, 41, 44, 45; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A survival study of patients after a heart attack used Cox proportional hazards regression, adjusted for age and sex. Patients with a ventricular arrhythmia had a hazard ratio for death of 3.0 compared with patients without one. How should this hazard ratio be read?","choices":["One third the risk of dying","A 3 percent risk of dying","Survival three years shorter","Three times the risk of dying"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because a hazard ratio can be interpreted as a relative risk, so a value of 3 means the group with the factor has three times the risk of the outcome. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In an unblinded trial of a new sleep medication against placebo, participants knew which pill they were taking. Many in the placebo group began using over-the-counter sleep aids during the study, and the drug showed little advantage. Which bias does this describe?","choices":["Selection bias","Performance bias","Non-response bias","Reporting bias"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because care other than the topic of interest differed between the groups, and blinding study participants is what protects against it. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 40, 44, 45"},{"q":"A published trial of a new arthritis drug listed six outcomes in its plan, including pain, function and stomach bleeding. The journal article reported only the two outcomes that favored the drug, and the other four were never made available. Which bias does this describe?","choices":["Reporting bias","Publication bias","Measurement bias","Attrition effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because only a subset of all the relevant data was made available, even though the trial itself was published. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 41, 44, 45, 46"},{"q":"Four hundred adults with knee osteoarthritis were placed by a computer-generated random sequence either in a supervised exercise program or in usual care. Pain scores were measured in both groups after 12 weeks. What feature makes this study experimental rather than observational?","choices":["The groups were followed forward in time","The investigators assigned the intervention","Two groups were compared on an outcome","Pain was recorded as a numerical score"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because in an experimental design the intervention (independent variable) is assigned and manipulated by the investigator, whereas in an observational design it is only observed. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 9, 25"},{"q":"A study of two blood tests for appendicitis reports that test A has a sensitivity of 98 percent and a specificity of 70 percent, while test B has a sensitivity of 75 percent and a specificity of 97 percent. A clinician wants a negative result to rule appendicitis out. Which choice is best?","choices":["Test B, as high specificity leaves few false negatives","Test A, as high sensitivity leaves few false negatives","Test B, as a positive result on it rules disease in","Test A, as high sensitivity leaves few false positives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a negative result on a highly sensitive test rules disease out: when sensitivity is high, there are very few false negatives. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A clinician scanning a list of new articles finds one whose title matches her question. She wants to learn quickly what the study was about and what it concluded. Which part of the article should she read first?","choices":["The methods","The abstract","The discussion","The figures and tables"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a quick reading starts with the abstract, which shows what the study is about and what was concluded. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"A reader has decided an article is relevant and now wants to see the actual size of the difference in outcomes between the groups, with the numbers for each group. Where should the reader look?","choices":["The figures and tables","The introduction","The methods","The title"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the question of how big the effect was is answered by the figures and tables during an in-depth review. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 39, 41"},{"q":"A national task force reviews a screening test for adults over 85 and concludes, with moderate certainty, that the service has no net benefit. Which grade of recommendation does this conclusion match?","choices":["Grade B","Grade C","Grade D","Grade I"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because grade D recommends against a service when there is moderate or high certainty that it has no net benefit or that the harms outweigh the benefits. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"Investigators followed 3,000 adults for ten years, comparing those who took a daily aspirin with those who did not, and counted the new cases of colon cancer in each group. Which measure of association should they report?","choices":["Odds ratio","Likelihood ratio","Case-fatality ratio","Relative risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because a cohort study can directly calculate the incidence of disease in the exposed and the non-exposed, so relative risk is used rather than the odds ratio. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 35, 40; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Investigators planning a study of a dietary supplement debate how to control confounding. One suggests matching participants on age and sex, and another suggests random assignment to supplement or placebo. Which advantage does random assignment offer over matching?","choices":["It makes the results generalizable","It can be applied after data are collected","It removes the need to blind anyone","It balances factors no one chose to measure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because randomization is the only method of dealing with differences between groups whose benefits are not limited to the factors selected for consideration. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 25, 26; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"An observational study reports that patients whose colon cancer was found by screening lived a median of 7 years after diagnosis, compared with 4 years for patients diagnosed after symptoms appeared. The tumors in both groups had similar growth rates, and patients in both groups died at about the same age. Which bias best explains the longer survival after diagnosis?","choices":["Length-time bias","Lead-time bias","Compliance bias","Overdiagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because screening moved the diagnosis earlier without moving the death, so the apparent gain is increased time knowing the diagnosis rather than improved survival. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 17, 18, 19, 21, 23"},{"q":"A trial at one referral hospital randomized patients, blinded the outcome assessors, kept the groups similar at baseline and followed every patient to the end. All participants were referred by specialists and had severe disease. Which description best fits the trial?","choices":["Low internal, high external validity","Low internal, uncertain external validity","High internal, uncertain external validity","High internal and high external validity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because its methods protect the correctness of the results for the patients studied, but referred patients with severe disease may not represent those in other clinical settings. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 26, 30"},{"q":"A survey of 3,000 adults asked, in one interview, about current depression and about exercise in the past week. Adults with depression reported less exercise. The authors conclude that inactivity causes depression. What is the main weakness of that conclusion?","choices":["It cannot measure how common depression is","It cannot include adults without depression","It cannot show which came first in time","It cannot use questionnaires to collect data"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because a cross-sectional study measures the risk factor and the condition at the same time, while causality requires that the cause precede the effect. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 15, 22, 23; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A county health department mailed one questionnaire to 2,400 randomly chosen adults during a single month. It asked whether each person currently smoked and whether each currently had a chronic cough, and both answers came from the same reply. No one was contacted again. Which study design is this?","choices":["Cross-sectional study","Prospective cohort study","Case-control study","Randomized controlled trial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the data were obtained at a single point in time, with the risk factor (smoking) and the condition (cough) measured at the same time in each person. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 9, 17, 22, 27, 30"}]};
